{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states. He was born around 1140 and became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father Raymond II was assassinated. Raymond III played a crucial role in the politics and military affairs of the region during the 12th century.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\n**Early Life and Reign**\nRaymond III was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. He succeeded his father at a young age and ruled under the regency of his mother until he came of age. His early reign was marked by conflicts with the Muslim forces led by Nur ad-Din who sought to expand his control over the Crusader states. Raymond III had to navigate these turbulent times while consolidating his power and maintaining the stability of Tripoli.\n\n**Political and Military Career**\nRaymond III is best known for his involvement in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a key player in the factional struggles that characterized the reign of King Baldwin IV the Leper. Raymond III became regent for Baldwin IV in 1174 and played a significant role in the governance of the kingdom. He was known for his diplomatic skills and efforts to maintain a balance of power between the various factions including the royal family the nobility and the military orders.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Conflict with Saladin**\nOne of the defining aspects of Raymond IIIs career was his conflict with Saladin the Muslim leader who sought to unify the Islamic world and expel the Crusaders. Raymond III led the Crusader forces at the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where they achieved a significant victory against Saladin. However the political landscape changed dramatically with the death of Baldwin IV in 1185 and the subsequent rise of Guy of Lusignan as king. Raymond IIIs influence waned as Guy pursued a more confrontational approach against Saladin leading to the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187.\n\n**Later Life and Legacy**\nAfter the Battle of Hattin Raymond III was captured by Saladin but was later released. He continued to play a role in the politics of the Crusader states until his death in 1187. His legacy is marked by his efforts to maintain the stability and sovereignty of the Crusader states during a period of increasing Muslim pressure. Raymond III is remembered as a capable leader who navigated the complex political and military landscape of his time with varying degrees of success.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\n**Family and Succession**\nRaymond III was married to Eschiva of Bures who was the Lady of Galilee. Their marriage was politically significant as it connected Raymond III to one of the powerful noble families in the Crusader states. They had no known children which led to a succession crisis upon Raymond IIIs death. His death marked the end of the original line of Counts of Tripoli and led to a period of instability and eventual Muslim conquest of the county.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.22343038022518158}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.2543639838695526}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -8.95221673999913e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.012625525705516338}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00010001159535022452}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -6.198863957251888e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.3245429992675781}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.047548435628414154}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1086402082582936e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00018487652414478362}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -1.806964635848999}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.510157047363464e-06}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -7.60526381782256e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.03284885734319687}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0553627870